S=[
("hook","Look down at your keyboard. Q, W, E, R, T, Y. Billions of people type on this arrangement every day: on laptops, on office desks, on the glass of their phones."),
("hook2","And yet it was designed in the 1870s, for a machine with no screen, no electricity, and no idea what an email was."),
("title","So why, after a century and a half of technological revolution, are we still using it? The real answer is stranger, and more human, than the story you've probably heard."),
("milwaukee","Our story begins in Milwaukee, Wisconsin, in the late 1860s. A newspaper editor and politician named Christopher Latham Sholes was tinkering in a machine shop with two collaborators, Carlos Glidden and Samuel Soule."),
("piano","Their early typewriter, patented in 1868, looked surprisingly like a piano. Two rows of keys, with the letters laid out in roughly alphabetical order."),
("evolve","It didn't stay that way. Over the next five years, backed by an investor named James Densmore, Sholes rebuilt the machine again and again, and the keys kept moving."),
("remington","In 1873, the design was sold to E. Remington and Sons, a company better known for guns and sewing machines. The Sholes and Glidden Type-Writer went on sale in 1874. It typed only capital letters, and its keyboard was already remarkably close to the one in front of you."),
("no2","A few keys still shuffled around, and in 1878 the Remington Number 2 added a shift key, so one machine could type both upper and lower case. QWERTY, more or less as we know it, had arrived."),
("myth","Now, here's the story you've probably heard. QWERTY was deliberately designed to slow typists down, so the machine wouldn't jam."),
("mech","There's a grain of truth in it. Early typewriters used typebars: metal arms arranged in a ring beneath the paper. Press a key, and its arm swings up to strike. Press two neighbouring keys too quickly, and the arms can collide and stick."),
("er","So separating some common letter pairs may well have helped. But look closely. E and R sit side by side, and E R is one of the most common pairs in English. If the goal was maximum separation, QWERTY does a strange job of it."),
("slow","And the idea of slowing typists down makes little commercial sense. Remington was selling speed. Nobody markets a machine by promising it will make you slower."),
("telegraph","Some researchers point somewhere else entirely. In a 2011 paper, Koichi and Motoko Yasuoka of Kyoto University argued that the layout was shaped by the typewriter's earliest professional users, telegraph operators transcribing Morse code, whose needs nudged keys into new positions."),
("verdict","That account is debated too. The honest answer is that QWERTY wasn't the product of one grand plan. It came from mechanical limits, feedback from users, and years of small, practical compromises. It was never designed to be the best possible layout. It just had to work."),
("sales","So how did a compromise become a global standard? The first reason is simple. Remington got there early, and sold a lot of machines."),
("contest","As typewriters spread into offices, typing became a profession, and schools taught the layout that businesses were buying. In a widely publicised contest in Cincinnati in 1888, a stenographer named Frank McGurrin, typing by touch on a Remington, beat a rival using a different machine. It was superb advertising."),
("trust","Then, in 1893, several of the biggest manufacturers, including Remington, combined to form the Union Typewriter Company, and QWERTY became the standard across their machines."),
("network","From there, it fed itself. Typists learned QWERTY because employers used it. Employers bought QWERTY because typists knew it. Schools taught it because everyone demanded it. Economists call this a network effect: the more people use something, the more valuable it becomes to everyone else."),
("computers","When typewriters gave way to teleprinters, then computer terminals, then personal computers and smartphones, each new technology inherited the old keyboard. Not because engineers proved it was ideal, but because millions of fingers already knew where the keys were."),
("path","In 1985, the economist Paul David made QWERTY his headline example of path dependence. That is, how an early, almost accidental choice can lock in an entire future."),
("dvorak","Which raises the obvious question. Could we do better? In 1936, education professor August Dvorak and his colleague William Dealey patented a layout built on studies of letter frequency. It puts all five vowels under the left hand's resting position, and many of the most common consonants under the right."),
("claims","For decades, Dvorak's layout was promoted as dramatically faster. But some of the most striking early evidence came from studies Dvorak himself was involved in. A 1956 study for the US government found that retraining experienced typists didn't pay off, and in 1990, economists Stan Liebowitz and Stephen Margolis argued the superiority claims had been overstated."),
("today","Today, many Dvorak users report less finger movement and greater comfort. But solid evidence for big speed gains is thin, and for most people the difference appears to be modest."),
("colemak","Then there's Colemak, released in 2006 by Shai Coleman. It's a gentler compromise. It moves seventeen keys, keeps shortcuts like Z, X, C and V where they are, and aims to reduce finger travel without forcing you to start from scratch."),
("tradeoff","And that's the real trade-off. A better layout might save you some effort. But switching means weeks of slow, frustrating typing, keyboards that don't match at work or on a friend's laptop, and muscle memory that fights you every step of the way. The cost arrives today. The benefit is small, and far away."),
("phones","Meanwhile, the ground is already shifting. On phones, autocorrect, swipe typing and voice input do much of the work. The layout matters less when you're barely pressing individual keys at all."),
("future","So, will we ever move beyond QWERTY? Probably not by replacing it. More likely, we'll gradually type less, and QWERTY will fade the way it arrived: slowly, and without anyone really deciding."),
("end","QWERTY survived not because it was perfect, but because it was good enough, and because everyone already knew it. Which leaves a question. What else in your life works exactly the same way? Tell us in the comments. This has been The Unnecessary Lab. Thanks for watching."),
]
